    <!-- =====================================================
         TEJADA — COCONUT
         ===================================================== -->
 
    <div id="tejadaScience"
         class="science-detail-page">
 
        <button class="science-back-btn"
                onclick="backToScienceMain()">
 
            ← Back
 
        </button>

 
        <div class="science-detail-header">
 
            <span>TEJADA'S TREE</span>
 
            <h2>Niyog / Coconut</h2>
 
            <p>
                <em>Cocos nucifera</em> L.
            </p>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- SPECIES PROFILE -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card">
 
            <h3>🌿 Species Profile</h3>
 
            <div class="science-info-grid">
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Common Name</strong>
                    <p>Niyog / Coconut</p>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Scientific Name</strong>
                    <p><em>Cocos nucifera</em> L.</p>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Family</strong>
                    <p>Arecaceae</p>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Origin</strong>
                    <p>
                        Central Malesia to the Southwest Pacific,
                        including the Philippines.
                    </p>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Conservation Status</strong>
                    <p>
                        Least Concern in the conservation assessment
                        reported by Kew's species information.
                    </p>
                </div>
 
            </div>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- BIOLOGY -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card">
 
            <h3>🧬 Biology</h3>
 
            <p>
                The coconut is a tropical palm with a single,
                generally slender and unbranched trunk. Its leaves
                are long and pinnate, forming a crown at the top
                of the trunk.
            </p>
 
            <div class="science-facts">
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Growth Rate</strong>
                    <span>
                        Growth varies depending on climate,
                        soil, water, nutrients, and cultivation.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Mature Height</strong>
                    <span>
                        Coconut palms can grow to several metres
                        in height and develop a tall trunk.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Root System</strong>
                    <span>
                        It develops a fibrous root system typical
                        of palms, with many roots extending from
                        the base of the trunk.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Leaf Structure</strong>
                    <span>
                        Leaves are pinnate, meaning many narrow
                        leaflets are arranged along a central
                        leaf stalk.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Bark / Trunk</strong>
                    <span>
                        The trunk is generally smooth, slender,
                        and unbranched.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Flowering / Fruiting</strong>
                    <span>
                        The coconut produces flowers in
                        inflorescences and develops the familiar
                        coconut fruit with a fibrous husk and
                        hard inner shell.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
            </div>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- ECOLOGY -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card">
 
            <h3>🌎 Ecology</h3>
 
            <p>
                Coconut is strongly associated with warm tropical
                environments and is commonly cultivated in coastal
                areas and other suitable tropical locations.
                It is also widely grown around human communities
                and agricultural areas.
            </p>
 
            <div class="science-facts">
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Habitat</strong>
                    <span>
                        Tropical coastal areas, plantations,
                        gardens, and other cultivated landscapes.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Soil Requirements</strong>
                    <span>
                        Performs well in suitable tropical soils
                        with adequate drainage and nutrients.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Water Requirements</strong>
                    <span>
                        Requires adequate moisture for healthy
                        growth, especially while establishing.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Sunlight</strong>
                    <span>
                        Coconut is adapted to sunny tropical
                        environments.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Native Range</strong>
                    <span>
                        Central Malesia to the Southwest Pacific,
                        including the Philippines.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
                <div>
                    <strong>Wildlife Interactions</strong>
                    <span>
                        Its flowers, fruits, and vegetation can
                        contribute resources to organisms within
                        tropical ecosystems.
                    </span>
                </div>
 
            </div>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- ENVIRONMENTAL BENEFITS -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card">
 
            <h3>🌱 Environmental Benefits</h3>
 
            <div class="benefit-grid">
 
                <div class="benefit-item">
                    <span>🌬️</span>
 
                    <h4>Carbon Sequestration</h4>
 
                    <p>
                        Through photosynthesis, the coconut tree
                        takes in carbon dioxide and incorporates
                        carbon into its growing plant tissues.
                    </p>
 
                </div>

 
                <div class="benefit-item">
                    <span>💨</span>
 
                    <h4>Oxygen Production</h4>
 
                    <p>
                        Photosynthesis releases oxygen as a
                        by-product while the plant produces
                        organic compounds for growth.
                    </p>
 
                </div>

 
                <div class="benefit-item">
                    <span>💧</span>
 
                    <h4>Watershed Protection</h4>
 
                    <p>
                        Vegetation can contribute to soil and
                        water protection by helping reduce the
                        direct impact of rainfall on exposed soil.
                    </p>
 
                </div>

 
                <div class="benefit-item">
                    <span>🌱</span>
 
                    <h4>Erosion Control</h4>
 
                    <p>
                        Plant roots can help hold soil particles
                        together and vegetation can provide
                        ground and soil protection.
                    </p>
 
                </div>

 
                <div class="benefit-item">
                    <span>🦋</span>
 
                    <h4>Biodiversity</h4>
 
                    <p>
                        Trees and palms can contribute habitat
                        structure and provide resources for
                        organisms living in the surrounding
                        environment.
                    </p>
 
                </div>
 
            </div>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- PHOTOSYNTHESIS -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card photosynthesis-card">
 
            <h3>☀️ Photosynthesis & Climate</h3>
 
            <p>
                Coconut leaves perform photosynthesis. During this
                process, the plant uses light energy, carbon dioxide,
                and water to produce chemical energy in the form of
                organic compounds. Oxygen is released as a
                by-product.
            </p>
 
            <div class="photosynthesis-flow">
 
                <div>
                    ☀️
                    <strong>Sunlight</strong>
                </div>
 
                <span>→</span>
 
                <div>
                    🌿
                    <strong>Leaves</strong>
                </div>
 
                <span>→</span>
 
                <div>
                    💨
                    <strong>CO₂</strong>
                </div>
 
                <span>→</span>
 
                <div>
                    🌱
                    <strong>Growth</strong>
                </div>
 
            </div>
 
            <p>
                Because plants remove carbon dioxide from the
                atmosphere during photosynthesis, growing vegetation
                participates in the global carbon cycle. Coconut
                vegetation can therefore contribute to carbon storage
                as part of its growth and biomass.
            </p>
 
        </div>

 
        <!-- REFERENCES -->
 
        <div class="science-info-card">
 
            <h3>📚 References</h3>
 
            <p class="reference-note">
                Scientific information is based on botanical and
                scientific reference sources. References will be
                presented in APA 7th edition format.
            </p>
 
            <div class="references-list">
 
                <p>
                    1. Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew.
                    <em>
                        Plants of the World Online:
                        Cocos nucifera L.
                    </em>
                </p>
 
                <p>
                    2. Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew.
                    <em>
                        Plants of the World Online:
                        Cocos nucifera L. — General Information.
                    </em>
                </p>
 
                <p>
                    3. Govaerts, R., Nic Lughadha, E., Black, N.,
                    Turner, R., & Paton, A. (2021).
                    The World Checklist of Vascular Plants.
                </p>
 
            </div>
 
        </div>
 
    </div>

 
